description:
Write lesson 6.02's asset cache, with the loading and unloading done by functions you pass in (in a real game they call `LoadTexture` and `UnloadTexture`):

```cpp
std::string normalizePath(const std::string& path);

class AssetCache
{
public:
    AssetCache(std::function<int(const std::string&)> load, std::function<void(int)> unload);
    ~AssetCache();                                   // unloads everything still loaded
    int acquire(const std::string& path);            // the asset's handle; loads it on first use
    void release(const std::string& path);           // one fewer user; unloads at zero
    int users(const std::string& path) const;        // 0 if not loaded
    std::size_t loaded() const;
};
```

**normalizePath**: backslashes become `/`; empty parts and `.` parts are removed; `..` removes the part before it (a `..` with nothing before it is kept); letters become lower case (Windows file names ignore case). So `Assets\\.\\sprites//../Player.PNG` is `assets/player.png`.

**AssetCache**: every function normalises the path first, so different spellings of the same file share one asset. `acquire` calls `load` only the first time (or again after the asset was fully released) and counts users. `release` on an asset that is not loaded does nothing. A `load` result of `0` means it failed: it is **not** cached, and `acquire` returns 0.

